            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Phase.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Phase.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-dz_loses_nothing",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE REFLECTION IS A BIJECTION: dz sends the ten digits onto ten distinct digits, so nothing is lost and every step can be undone. Reversible, and therefore barren on its own."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 10).map dz).eraseDups.length = 10"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem dz_loses_nothing : ((List.range 10).map dz).eraseDups.length = 10 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-doubling_collapses_nine",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "DOUBLING LOSES A DIGIT, and the loss is named: 2·0 mod 9 = 0 and 2·9 mod 9 = 0, so nine and zero share an image. The map sends ten digits onto nine, is therefore not injective, and the step cannot be undone."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length = 9) ∧ (dbl 0 = dbl 9) ∧ (dbl 0 = 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem doubling_collapses_nine : (((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length = 9) ∧ (dbl 0 = dbl 9) ∧ (dbl 0 = 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-maps_differ_in_reach",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE TWO HALVES ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF MAP, and the line says so: the reflection's image has ten members and doubling's has nine, and ten is not nine. One erases nothing, the other erases exactly one digit per pass."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(((List.range 10).map dz).eraseDups.length ≠ ((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length) ∧ (10 ≠ 9)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem maps_differ_in_reach : (((List.range 10).map dz).eraseDups.length ≠ ((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length) ∧ (10 ≠ 9) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-zero_closes_in_phase",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "ONLY ZERO CLOSES. Both maps fix it — dz 0 = 0 and dbl 0 = 0 — so the walk returns to its seed after one completed pair, in phase, at two steps. It is the sole seed with a period, and the reason is that neither map moves it."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(dz 0 = 0) ∧ (dbl 0 = 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem zero_closes_in_phase : (dz 0 = 0) ∧ (dbl 0 = 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-five_returns_out_of_phase",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND A RETURN IS NOT A PERIOD. Five is fixed by the reflection (dz 5 = 5) so the walk sits on its seed after the FIRST step — but that is an odd number of operations, with doubling still owed, so the walk is out of phase and has not closed. Doubling then moves it: dbl 5 = 1, and 1 is not 5."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(dz 5 = 5) ∧ (dbl 5 = 1) ∧ (dbl 5 ≠ 5)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem five_returns_out_of_phase : (dz 5 = 5) ∧ (dbl 5 = 1) ∧ (dbl 5 ≠ 5) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-reach_shrinks_each_pass",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE DOMAIN NARROWS AS THE WALK RUNS: applying doubling to the ten digits leaves nine, and applying it again leaves nine of those — the image cannot grow. SCOPE: what decides here is that the image never widens, which is what makes a return to an outside seed impossible. That the walk therefore NEVER closes for such a seed is the reading— `by decide` settles the maps."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.map dbl).eraseDups.length ≤ ((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length) ∧ (((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length = 9)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem reach_shrinks_each_pass : ((((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.map dbl).eraseDups.length ≤ ((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length) ∧ (((List.range 10).map dbl).eraseDups.length = 9) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
